        <p>This guide serves two readers. Designers get the style-to-fabric logic and the hand-feel trade-offs. Brand owners and sourcing leads get the parameter checks, the failure points and the questions to ask before a PO goes out. The notes below come from the issues that keep recurring across hoodie, tee, jacket, pant, activewear and streetwear programs on the factory floor</p>
        
        <h2 id="why-fabric">Why Fabric Choice Makes or Breaks a Streetwear Drop</h2>
        <p>A customer judges a streetwear garment in about three seconds: how it hangs, how it feels, how it holds shape on a moving body. Pattern cutting sets proportion. Fabric sets character. A 400 GSM brushed fleece and a 280 GSM loopback can share the identical block and read as two unrelated products on the rack</p>
        <p>Fabric also decides whether the drop earns a second order. The complaints that kill repeat purchase are boringly consistent: the hem twists after one wash, the chest print cracks along the ink edge, the cuffs pill inside a month, the garment-dyed black fades to charcoal. None of those are design failures. Every one of them was locked in at sampling</p>
        <p><strong>Three things fabric decides before anything else:</strong></p>
        <ul class="ar-list">
        <li><strong>Silhouette behavior.</strong> Drape, structure, and whether an oversized cut falls or balloons</li>
        <li><strong>Print and wash compatibility.</strong> Whether a screen, puff or discharge print sits flat on the surface, and whether a garment dye distorts the body</li>
        <li><strong>Durability perception.</strong> Pilling, shrinkage and color retention are the metrics customers actually use to decide whether a brand is serious</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Read the style sections for what to specify. Read the performance and sourcing sections for how to verify it</p>
        
        <h2 id="sub-genre">Streetwear Is Not One Style: Match Fabric to Sub-Genre</h2>
        <p>Treating streetwear as a single aesthetic is the fastest route to the wrong material. An oversized hoodie, a cargo pant, a vintage-wash tee and a technical shell each run on different fabric logic. The table maps the main sub-genres to their fabric families and the GSM bands most mills quote against. Check them against your own approved sample and the qualities your supplier actually stocks before you commit</p>
        <p><strong>Table 1: Style-to-Fabric Mapping</strong></p>
        <div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table">
        <thead>
        <tr><th>Sub-genre</th><th>Primary fabric families</th><th>Typical GSM range (knit/woven)</th><th>Key structural note</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr><td>Oversized hoodie / sweatshirt</td><td>French terry, brushed fleece, loopback</td><td>280–450 GSM</td><td>Loopback for drape, fleece for warmth and structure</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Workwear / cargo pants</td><td>Cotton canvas, twill, ripstop</td><td>240–380 GSM (woven)</td><td>Canvas holds shape; ripstop adds tear resistance</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Vintage-wash / heavyweight tee</td><td>Heavyweight cotton jersey, garment-dyed jersey</td><td>180–260 GSM</td><td>Compact yarn resists distortion after dye</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Techwear / functional layers</td><td>Stretch woven, nylon, bonded softshell</td><td>120–220 GSM (woven)</td><td>Focus on stretch direction and DWR finish</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Skate / everyday graphic tee</td><td>Cotton single jersey, cotton-poly blend</td><td>150–200 GSM</td><td>Balanced print surface and cost</td></tr>
        </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <h3>Oversized Hoodies and Sweatshirts: French Terry vs. Fleece vs. Loopback</h3>
        <p>The oversized hoodie is the signature piece, and the three-way choice between French terry, brushed fleece and loopback decides how it behaves on body</p>
        <p>French terry runs a smooth face with a looped back. It drapes, it breathes better than fleece, and it suits mid-weight oversized cuts around 280–340 GSM where you want volume without board-stiffness. Brushed fleece is napped on the inside, warmer, fuller, and gives real structure at 380 GSM and up. Loopback sits between them: an unbrushed looped back with a clean, slightly drier hand, and still the default for high-end sweatshirt programs</p>
        <p>Working rule: loopback or French terry for a year-round drop, brushed fleece for cold-weather capsules. For heavyweight programs, <a href="/hoodie-manufacturer/">custom heavyweight hoodie manufacturing</a> covers the construction that has to pair with these fabrics, from rib density to shoulder taping</p>
        <h3>Workwear and Cargo: Canvas, Ripstop, and Twill</h3>
        <p>Cargo and workwear silhouettes need fabric that holds a shape and takes abrasion. Cotton canvas around 300–380 GSM gives the stiff, structured utility read, but it needs an enzyme or stone wash so it doesn't arrive feeling like cardboard. Twill is the wearable middle ground: a diagonal weave with better drape and a cleaner surface for garment dye. Ripstop, with its reinforcing grid, wins when tear resistance matters and you want a lighter hand around 200–240 GSM</p>
        <p>The trade-off is weight against comfort and freight. Heavy canvas photographs beautifully and keeps patch pockets flat. Lighter twill moves better, wrinkles less in transit and costs less to ship. Decide what the silhouette is selling, then fix the GSM</p>
        <h3>Vintage-Wash and Heavyweight Tees: Garment-Dyed Jersey</h3>
        <p>The vintage tee is a finish problem more than a fiber problem. Heavyweight cotton jersey at 180–260 GSM delivers the substantial hand high-end tees rely on, and compact-spun yarn keeps the face smooth enough for crisp print edges. Garment dyeing, where the finished piece goes into the bath instead of the greige roll, produces the slightly uneven washed tone that defines the category</p>
        <p>Garment dye is also where shrinkage and shade variation enter your program. The body will move during dyeing, so specify a pre-shrunk base fabric and a post-wash measurement tolerance before sampling closes. For print-heavy drops, <a href="/t-shirt-manufacturer/">custom t-shirt printing and manufacturing</a> explains how fabric choice interacts with screen, puff and discharge techniques</p>
        <h3>Techwear and Functional Layers: Stretch Woven and Softshell</h3>
        <p>Techwear shifts the conversation from hand-feel to measurable fields. Break the spec into parts: stretch direction (two-way or four-way), elongation percentage, recovery after relaxation, and support. "High stretch" is marketing language, not a specification. Get the direction and the recovery target in writing</p>
        <p>Nylon and polyester stretch wovens at 120–220 GSM with a DWR finish are the usual base. Bonded softshell buys wind resistance and gives up breathability, which is a fair trade on a shell and a bad one on a mid-layer. On functional pieces the fabric choice and the seam construction are one decision, so pull your factory in during the first sample round rather than after</p>
        
        <h2 id="comparison">The Core Streetwear Fabrics Compared (Performance Table)</h2>
        <p>With the sub-genre fixed, compare candidates on the dimensions that generate returns and complaints. Ratings below are qualitative; cost tiers are relative planning signals, not quotations</p>
        <p><strong>Table 2: Fabric Performance Comparison</strong></p>
        <div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table">
